<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#induna">Glossary</a> · Term · title</p><h1 class="enc-title">Induna</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as iNduna, iziNduna, Nduna</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Induna, plural iziNduna, is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulu</a> and Xhosa title meaning advisor, headman, ambassador or commander of a group of warriors. It was reserved for senior officials appointed by the king or chief for their leadership, bravery or service, and the iziNduna often acted as a bridge between the people and the king, gathering in an indaba to discuss important matters. Among other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bantu-peoples">Bantu peoples</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> a nduna was the monarch's political representative and royal councillor. Notable holders include <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ndlela-kasompisi">Ndlela kaSompisi</a>, chief advisor to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dingane">Dingane</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ntshingwayo-khoza">Ntshingwayo</a> kaMahole, who commanded at Isandlwana. The word survives in South African English for a boss.</p></div>
